Adam Thornton wrote:
> AD&D v1 uses 6-second segments, 1-minute rounds, and 10-minute turns.
> However, the Nethack turn seems to most closely map to the AD&D round.

Nethack's "turn" seems to be kind of flexible in length.  It'd
certainly take some time to prepare and consume a dead monster corpse,
more than the handful of turns it takes in the game.  But that's beside
the point anyway, the point is, a gelatinous cube can kill a character
in three turns.

Even if you accept that a 'cube is supposed to be highly dangerous,
there are ways to get paralyzed for a handful of turns that could allow
an opportunistic gelatinous cube, unseen by the player just beforehand,
to quickly consume him, and early on too.

I say the suffocation period must be at least five turns.

